    "value": "Here's what you need to know about the relevant changes in Flask 3.1.0b2 (and the general 3.1.0/3.1.x cycle), especially concerning compatibility, request JSON handling, `get_json` behavior, and the `View` class:\n\n### 1. Compatibility\n- **Python Compatibility**: Flask 3.1.x drops support for Python 3.8; you need at least Python 3.9 now.\n- **Dependencies**: Required versions increased: Werkzeug \u003e= 3.1, ItsDangerous \u003e= 2.2, Blinker \u003e= 1.9【3:1†source】.\n\n### 2. JSON Request Handling and `get_json` Behavior\n- The `request.get_json()` method is unchanged in basics: it parses incoming JSON request data if the mimetype is `application/json`. You can force parsing with `force=True` even if the content type isn't JSON, but you should only do this if you’re sure the data is JSON.\n- If parsing fails, `on_json_loading_failed()` on the request object is invoked. By default, it will result in an error, unless `silent=True` is passed, in which case you'll get `None` instead of an exception.\n- In practice, this means for best compatibility, ensure your clients use `Content-Type: application/json`, and check for errors when using `force=True`【3:8†source】【3:7†source】.\n- There was some background change regarding `flask.json_available` for extension compatibility, but it’s merely a deprecated compatibility workaround【3:1†source】.\n\n### 3. The `View` Class and Class-Based Views\n- There were **no breaking changes** to `flask.views.View` or `MethodView`; the API and usage remain the same as in previous releases【3:6†source】.\n- You can continue to use `.as_view()` to create reusable class-based views, passing arguments to the constructor as needed.\n- All captured URL variables are passed as kwargs to `dispatch_request()`.\n\n### 4. Changelog/Release Notes for 3.1.0 (and beta)\n- The release mainly focused on updated dependencies, dropped support for older Python, and configuration enhancements.\n- There are some minor tweaks to app and request context (e.g., `RequestContext` merges with `AppContext`, but that's coming in 3.2.0).\n- No significant behavioral change to request JSON access or class-based views in 3.1.0b2.\n\n**Summary**: For Flask 3.1.0b2, check Python and dependency versions for compatibility. Code using `request.get_json()`, `request.json`, and class-based views (View, MethodView) should not break, but you should test your app to ensure all client requests use the correct `Content-Type`. There is no change in how you use these features, only minor internal adjustments and deprecation warnings for old, obscure behaviors.\n\n**References**:\n- Flask 3.1.x changes: [Official Flask Changelog](https://flask.palletsprojects.com/en/3.1.x/changes/)【3:1†source】\n- JSON request handling: [Flask Request.get_json Documentation](https://flask.palletsprojects.com/en/3.1.x/api/#flask.Request.get_json)【3:8†source】\n- Class-based views: [Flask Class-based Views Docs](https://flask.palletsprojects.com/en/3.1.x/views/)【3:6†source】",
